{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560109258,"sessionID":"ses_e2be2a3a9ffeGLGI0J0gF3MK6h","part":{"partID":"prt_1d41e28b3001rXXRxQ3T8EefyR","sessionID":"ses_e2be2a3a9ffeGLGI0J0gF3MK6h","messageID":"msg_1d41e0b300018OXZm77P4ktcyl","type":"tool","id":"call_80a17dc2582a427cb8bc9016","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown","offset":95,"limit":30},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown, lines 95-124\n95: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Compares compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph representations without writing. |\n96: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | The `/doctor:sk-skill check` engine. With no argument it prints one row per direct child of `.skilled/skills`, and with a skill name or a path one line per row `C1` to `C13`, each carrying its own status and detail. A skill inside another repository's `.skilled/skills` is read against that repository, and each of its rows names the repository it read. A tool that repository does not ship runs from the checker's own copy, and the row names that copy too. It calls the format gate, the root-metadata gate, the derived-block regeneration in dry-run form, the router-equivalence replay, the advisor graph and mirror checks and the description audit, and writes nothing. `--into-hub <hub>` prints the eleven surfaces a skill or mode must land on under that hub instead, each with the probe it ran, and still writes nothing. `--json` prints either report and nothing else. It exits 0 when no row is a finding, 1 when one is, and 2 when a precondition it needs is missing, with the provision command on its own line. |\n97: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | Plans, and on request applies, the conversion of one skill. `plan` reads, refuses and prints every step, `apply` prints the same plan and writes only with `--apply`, and `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves a reshaped body kept every original line. An older Skilled skill converts in place, with its machine router moved verbatim into a new sibling `ROUTER.md`. A plain skill reached through a path outside the skills root is staged under its frontmatter name and becomes a class S standalone root, and it converts only once the operator approves at least eight intent phrases, which become its `intent_signals`. A skill of neither shape is out of scope. Preflight refuses a hub declaration, a nested packet tree, a `local-` or `agent-` prefixed name, a name collision and a description over 130 characters. A skill that declares no `allowed-tools` field prints `allowed-tools: approval required; propose [...]` in its plan, and the apply writes no grant until a list is passed. A field declared twice is refused as `ALLOWED_TOOLS_DUPLICATE` rather than merged. The plain path keeps its agent output, its preservation snapshot and its staging copy in one scratch root beside the backup at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, deleted when a question is declined and once the run has reported. A skill whose router already lives in `ROUTER.md` gets its frontmatter normalized and nothing else. Every write is preceded by a backup under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/`, `ROUTER.md` is created exclusively, and a parsed router that would change restores the backup. After both format gates it runs the integration rows: the advisor scan, the compiled skill graph, the reach probe with the budget row, the regression cases and the changelog file and link. |\n98: | `sk-code-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the sk-code setup battery from a repository root: one verdict per setup leg, one repair command per failing leg and no writes. Its quality leg runs the quality runner's own tests, which build throwaway repositories under the OS temp folder and remove them. |\n99: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n100: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n101: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every canonical agent reaches all five runtime surfaces, and that mirror surfaces stay symlinked rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n102: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Three of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block and the repo-rules checker's summary. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n103: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. |\n104: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n105: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n106: | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n107: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the read-only sk-git setup battery for `/doctor:git setup`: eleven checks, each PASS, FAIL, SKIP, UNKNOWN or DEGRADED with the source it read and one repair per failure. It exits 0 when every check passes or skips, 3 when none failed but a check is UNKNOWN or DEGRADED, 1 on any failure and 2 on a usage error. It loads every helper from its own install root and never runs code from the repository it inspects. |\n108: | `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Compares every command catalog and hub metadata entry back to the command's own frontmatter, which is the one copy nothing else derives from. Structural drift fails. Prose divergence reports and fails only under `--strict`. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n109: | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n110: | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n111: | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | The read-only Codex approval check: it finds the advisor's `UserPromptSubmit` entry in `.codex/hooks.json` and reports whether the Codex config holds a `trusted_hash` for that exact group and index, because Codex skips an unapproved entry without a warning. `--root <dir>` reads another checkout, `--config <path>` reads another config and `--json` prints one object. It prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks`, `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` or `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` with the `/hooks` repair line, and never prints a hash. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n112: | `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is present for each runtime folder it finds. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n113: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n114: | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n115: | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n116: | `ci-toggle.cjs` | Plans one switch change for `/doctor:ci toggle`: the allowlist, the refusals from the live ruleset, `neverOff` and the README, the no-op check, the exact `gh variable` command and the second named confirmation for a protected workflow. It never changes a variable. |\n117: | `lib/ci-profile.cjs` | Finds, validates and normalizes the repository CI profile (`--profile`, then `git config skcode.ciProfile`, then `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/repository/ci.json`, then a generic default that forces nothing), rejecting an unknown key. A profile whose `repository` field names another repository is ignored, and one left at `.sk-code/ci.json` is refused. |\n118: | `lib/ci-report-sources.cjs` | Reads what the report takes from outside the workflow files: the repository variables through `gh variable list`, the branch the repository integrates on and the newest commit on that branch that is not a trigger-index rebuild. Arguments go in as an array and never through a shell, a variable value is reduced to a state and never printed, and a read that fails is reported as unknown with its reason. |\n119: | `lib/ci-report-workflows.cjs` | Reads the workflow files themselves: discovery under `.github/workflows/`, the YAML parse, the switch state per job, the drift flags, the purpose column read from the workflows README and the switch names the report lists. A switch is recognized by its shape, so any repository's switch names are read and none is assumed. |\n120: | `lib/ci-report-render.cjs` | Builds the report document and its two renderings, the JSON a machine reads and the fixed-width table a person reads, both from the same readings. The not-checked list travels with the document, so a clean row is never read as a pass for a rule no parser checks. |\n121: | `lib/ci-report-text.cjs` | `firstLine()`, the one-line rule a `gh` failure and a YAML parse error both need, because a whole message would break the table row, the JSON field and the stderr line that carry it. |\n122: | `lib/quality-skill-paths.cjs` | The one sk-code quality folder this doctor reads, and the handles inside it that answer every mode's question. Each path resolves from the file's own location, so the doctor works from a checkout anywhere, and the runner, the config reader and the helpers load here so every mode reads one skill folder, one config schema and the one runner the gate itself starts. |\n123: | `lib/quality-script-io.cjs` | The two ways the quality doctor answers whoever started it: the typed request failure that carries the exit code and the `STATUS=` error code a route reads, and `emit()`, the single place a report reaches stdout. One function writes the last line, so a route reads one whatever the mode. |\n124: | `lib/quality-config-source.cjs` | Which repository config a mode read, whether the runner trusts it and which core hygiene rules it switches off, every answer read through the module the gate itself reads so a doctor verdict and a gate verdict cannot drift. It also builds the suggestion an init prints for a repository with no `specs/` folder. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 125]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown, lines 95-124\n95: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Compares compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph representations without writing. |\n96: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | The `/doctor:sk-skill check` engine. With no argument it prints one row per direct child of `.skilled/skills`, and with a skill name or a path one line per row `C1` to `C13`, each carrying its own status and detail. A skill inside another repository's `.skilled/skills` is read against that repository, and each of its rows names the repository it read. A tool that repository does not ship runs from the checker's own copy, and the row names that copy too. It calls the format gate, the root-metadata gate, the derived-block regeneration in dry-run form, the router-equivalence replay, the advisor graph and mirror checks and the description audit, and writes nothing. `--into-hub <hub>` prints the eleven surfaces a skill or mode must land on under that hub instead, each with the probe it ran, and still writes nothing. `--json` prints either report and nothing else. It exits 0 when no row is a finding, 1 when one is, and 2 when a precondition it needs is missing, with the provision command on its own line. |\n97: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | Plans, and on request applies, the conversion of one skill. `plan` reads, refuses and prints every step, `apply` prints the same plan and writes only with `--apply`, and `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves a reshaped body kept every original line. An older Skilled skill converts in place, with its machine router moved verbatim into a new sibling `ROUTER.md`. A plain skill reached through a path outside the skills root is staged under its frontmatter name and becomes a class S standalone root, and it converts only once the operator approves at least eight intent phrases, which become its `intent_signals`. A skill of neither shape is out of scope. Preflight refuses a hub declaration, a nested packet tree, a `local-` or `agent-` prefixed name, a name collision and a description over 130 characters. A skill that declares no `allowed-tools` field prints `allowed-tools: approval required; propose [...]` in its plan, and the apply writes no grant until a list is passed. A field declared twice is refused as `ALLOWED_TOOLS_DUPLICATE` rather than merged. The plain path keeps its agent output, its preservation snapshot and its staging copy in one scratch root beside the backup at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, deleted when a question is declined and once the run has reported. A skill whose router already lives in `ROUTER.md` gets its frontmatter normalized and nothing else. Every write is preceded by a backup under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/`, `ROUTER.md` is created exclusively, and a parsed router that would change restores the backup. After both format gates it runs the integration rows: the advisor scan, the compiled skill graph, the reach probe with the budget row, the regression cases and the changelog file and link. |\n98: | `sk-code-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the sk-code setup battery from a repository root: one verdict per setup leg, one repair command per failing leg and no writes. Its quality leg runs the quality runner's own tests, which build throwaway repositories under the OS temp folder and remove them. |\n99: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n100: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n101: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every canonical agent reaches all five runtime surfaces, and that mirror surfaces stay symlinked rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n102: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Three of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block and the repo-rules checker's summary. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n103: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. |\n104: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n105: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n106: | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n107: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the read-only sk-git setup battery for `/doctor:git setup`: eleven checks, each PASS, FAIL, SKIP, UNKNOWN or DEGRADED with the source it read and one repair per failure. It exits 0 when every check passes or skips, 3 when none failed but a check is UNKNOWN or DEGRADED, 1 on any failure and 2 on a usage error. It loads every helper from its own install root and never runs code from the repository it inspects. |\n108: | `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Compares every command catalog and hub metadata entry back to the command's own frontmatter, which is the one copy nothing else derives from. Structural drift fails. Prose divergence reports and fails only under `--strict`. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n109: | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n110: | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n111: | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | The read-only Codex approval check: it finds the advisor's `UserPromptSubmit` entry in `.codex/hooks.json` and reports whether the Codex config holds a `trusted_hash` for that exact group and index, because Codex skips an unapproved entry without a warning. `--root <dir>` reads another checkout, `--config <path>` reads another config and `--json` prints one object. It prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks`, `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` or `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` with the `/hooks` repair line, and never prints a hash. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n112: | `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is present for each runtime folder it finds. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n113: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n114: | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n115: | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n116: | `ci-toggle.cjs` | Plans one switch change for `/doctor:ci toggle`: the allowlist, the refusals from the live ruleset, `neverOff` and the README, the no-op check, the exact `gh variable` command and the second named confirmation for a protected workflow. It never changes a variable. |\n117: | `lib/ci-profile.cjs` | Finds, validates and normalizes the repository CI profile (`--profile`, then `git config skcode.ciProfile`, then `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/repository/ci.json`, then a generic default that forces nothing), rejecting an unknown key. A profile whose `repository` field names another repository is ignored, and one left at `.sk-code/ci.json` is refused. |\n118: | `lib/ci-report-sources.cjs` | Reads what the report takes from outside the workflow files: the repository variables through `gh variable list`, the branch the repository integrates on and the newest commit on that branch that is not a trigger-index rebuild. Arguments go in as an array and never through a shell, a variable value is reduced to a state and never printed, and a read that fails is reported as unknown with its reason. |\n119: | `lib/ci-report-workflows.cjs` | Reads the workflow files themselves: discovery under `.github/workflows/`, the YAML parse, the switch state per job, the drift flags, the purpose column read from the workflows README and the switch names the report lists. A switch is recognized by its shape, so any repository's switch names are read and none is assumed. |\n120: | `lib/ci-report-render.cjs` | Builds the report document and its two renderings, the JSON a machine reads and the fixed-width table a person reads, both from the same readings. The not-checked list travels with the document, so a clean row is never read as a pass for a rule no parser checks. |\n121: | `lib/ci-report-text.cjs` | `firstLine()`, the one-line rule a `gh` failure and a YAML parse error both need, because a whole message would break the table row, the JSON field and the stderr line that carry it. |\n122: | `lib/quality-skill-paths.cjs` | The one sk-code quality folder this doctor reads, and the handles inside it that answer every mode's question. Each path resolves from the file's own location, so the doctor works from a checkout anywhere, and the runner, the config reader and the helpers load here so every mode reads one skill folder, one config schema and the one runner the gate itself starts. |\n123: | `lib/quality-script-io.cjs` | The two ways the quality doctor answers whoever started it: the typed request failure that carries the exit code and the `STATUS=` error code a route reads, and `emit()`, the single place a report reaches stdout. One function writes the last line, so a route reads one whatever the mode. |\n124: | `lib/quality-config-source.cjs` | Which repository config a mode read, whether the runner trusts it and which core hygiene rules it switches off, every answer read through the module the gate itself reads so a doctor verdict and a gate verdict cannot drift. It also builds the suggestion an init prints for a repository with no `specs/` folder. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 125]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\",\"offset\":95,\"limit\":30}"},"time":{"start":1794560108723,"end":1794560109258}}}}
